Fixed. The bug was in `insert_index` at `repo/search.py:8` — the binary search used `sorted_list[mid] < x`, which returns the leftmost insertion point, so duplicate keys were inserted *before* existing equal items. Changing it to `<=` makes it skip past equal elements, giving the rightmost insertion point:

```4:12:/Users/psudokit/benchmark/runs/20261016-104535-g2-kimi-k3-T040-bisect-vague-prompt-r0/work/repo/search.py
def insert_index(sorted_list, x):
    lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_list)
    while lo < hi:
        mid = (lo + hi) // 2
        if sorted_list[mid] <= x:
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid
    return lo
```

Both visible tests pass (`insert_index([1, 2, 2, 3], 2) == 3`, `insert_index([1, 3, 5], 4) == 2`). The legacy `old_insert_index` delegates to `insert_index`, so it picks up the fix too.

